INT. DERELICT BASEBALL STADIUM - HOME DUGOUT - NIGHT

A cavern of concrete and shadow. Rows of empty seats rise behind the dugout like an audience of the dead. A single stadium light flickers over the infield, where weeds have pushed through the dirt.

COACH LEONARD "LEFTY" OKONKWO-REYES (70s, faded Cubs-blue cap, hands like old leather gloves) sits on the bench with a bat across his knees. Beside him, his former star pitcher, MARCUS "THE ARM" TRENT (40s, scarred right elbow, expensive coat), stares at the empty mound. And a sports radio crackles from a battered transistor on the bench.

                    ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
          (over radio)
          ...and that's the final out. The Wolves take the series, four games to one.

Lefty turns the dial. The voice dies in static.

                    LEFTY
          Forty years I listened to that.

                    MARCUS
          Coach, you didn't have to come out here.

                    LEFTY
          You called me.

                    MARCUS
          I called to say I'm sorry.

                    LEFTY
          (not looking at him)
          Then say it.

Marcus opens his mouth. Closes it. He stands and walks to the dugout steps.

                    MARCUS
          I sold the game, Lefty.

Lefty doesn't flinch. The stadium light buzzes. A bat taps a slow rhythm on the concrete.

                    LEFTY
          I know.

                    MARCUS
          You know?

                    LEFTY
          I've known since the sixth inning.

Marcus leans on the rail. His breath comes out ragged. Lefty rises, joints popping, and joins him.

                    LEFTY (CONT'D)
          You threw a curve on 3-and-2.

                    MARCUS
          Coach--

                    LEFTY
          You never threw a curve on 3-and-2. Not once in eleven years.

Marcus slides down the rail and sits on the top step, head bowed. Lefty looks out across the field. The infield dirt seems to shimmer in the flicker.

                    LEFTY (CONT'D)
          You think I came out here to hit you?

                    MARCUS
          I think you should.

                    LEFTY
          I came out here to hit you a few grounders.

He pulls a baseball from his coat pocket and holds it out. Marcus stares at it for a long moment. Then he takes it.

                    MARCUS
          I'm forty years old.

                    LEFTY
          I'm seventy-four.

He picks up the bat. Marcus gets to his feet slowly. Lefty gestures at the mound.

                    LEFTY (CONT'D)
          Go on. Take your spot.

Marcus walks out of the dugout onto the field. The weeds brush his coat. He climbs the mound and turns to face home plate.

Lefty steps out of the dugout, takes a stance at the empty plate, and lifts the bat.

                    LEFTY (CONT'D)
          (calling)
          Fastball, Arm. Right down the middle.

Marcus winds up. He throws. The ball cracks off the bat and sails into the darkness. They both watch it go.

                    MARCUS
          (softly)
          Foul?

                    LEFTY
          Gone.

They stand there, the old man and the young one, as the stadium light gives one last flicker and the radio on the bench, left on by no one, murmurs a single faint syllable.

                    ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
          ...next.

Lefty lowers the bat. Marcus lowers his arm.
